"GitHub - tadamcz/gamma-theta: A Lean-verified candidate counterexample to the γ–θ conjecture in eternal domination. · GitHub (https://github.com/tadamcz/gamma-theta)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; A Lean-verified candidate counterexample to the γ–θ conjecture in eternal domination.\n# A counterexample to the γ–θ conjecture\n\nGammaThetaCounterexample.lean proves that an explicit graph on 243 vertices satisfies\n\n$$ \\gamma(G)=\\gamma^{\\infty}(G)=3<\\theta(G). $$\n\nHere γ is the domination number, γ∞ is the eternal domination number in the one-guard-moves model, and θ is the vertex clique-covering number. The graph is the complement of the ternary Golay Cayley graph described in the file. This is a counterexample to the universal γ–θ conjecture, answering Klostermeyer–Mynhardt's Question 7.1 affirmatively.\n\nThe cleaned proof contains no `sorry` declarations.\n\nThe result is written up in Tom Adamczewski and William F. Klostermeyer, A Counterexample to an Eternal Domination Conjecture, arXiv:2609.11500 [math.CO], 10 September 2026. The paper establishes γ(G) = γ∞(G) = 3 by exhaustive computation over all 1,987,821 dominating triples and bounds ω(G) ≤ 45 via the Delsarte–Hoffman bound, hence θ(G) ≥ 5, sharpening the θ(G) > 3 proved in Lean here. As the paper's contribution statement records, the mathematics of the paper is Klostermeyer's; Adamczewski's contribution was limited to setting up and running the experiment in which GPT-6 Astra found the graph.\n\n## Informal summary of the argument\n\nStart with a graph whose local structure is very rigid. Let H be the ternary Golay graph. Its vertices are the 243 vectors of length five over the integers modulo three. Two vertices are adjacent when their difference belongs to a fixed set of 22 vectors: the eleven generators displayed in the proof and their negatives. A finite calculation establishes three facts: every vertex has 22 neighbors, every adjacent pair has exactly one common neighbor, and every distinct nonadjacent pair has exactly two. The counterexample is G = Hᶜ, so two distinct vertices are adjacent in G precisely when they are not adjacent in H.\n\nThree guards are necessary and suffice for ordinary domination. A set of vertices dominates G exactly when no vertex of H is adjacent to every member of the set. That same vertex would then neighbor all three original guards, contradicting the original triple's domination.\n  * No H-neighbors. All three moves are legal. Suppose all three resulting triples failed. Let p, q, s respectively be common H-neighbors of {r, b, c}, {r, a, c}, and {r, a, b}. They are distinct, since any coincidence would already give a common neighbor of the original triple. The Cayley structure and common-neighbor counts give a parallelogram identity: two distinct common neighbors r and c of p and q satisfy c = p + q − r. Applying this to the three pairs gives a = q + s − r, b = p + s − r, and c = p + q − r. Now z = p + q + s − 2r has differences p − r, q − r, and s − r from a, b, and c. These are all allowed edge directions, so z neighbors every original guard in H: again a contradiction.\n\nThus every attack admits a legal move to another dominating triple. The same argument applies after every move, giving an indefinite defense and proving γ∞(G) = 3.\n\nThree cliques cannot cover G. Such a cover would give a proper three-coloring of H. Around any vertex v, the 22 neighbors form eleven disjoint edges: each neighbor has exactly one neighbor within this neighborhood. Each edge must use the two colors different from v's, so every vertex has eleven neighbors of each other color. Counting edges between a color class C and its complement gives 22|C| = 11(243 − |C|), hence |C| = 81.\n\nFinally, take v in C and count edges between its neighborhood and C ∖ {v}. Each of v's 22 neighbors has ten neighbors in C other than v, giving 22 × 10 edges. But each of the other 80 vertices in C is nonadjacent to v and therefore has exactly two neighbors in v's neighborhood, giving 80 × 2 edges. These counts disagree. Hence H is not three-colorable, so θ(G) = χ(H) > 3.\n\n## Original benchmark material\n\nThe original files are retained for provenance and are not build targets:\n\n  * Challenge.lean: the benchmark statement and placeholders.\n  * submission/Spec.lean: the unchanged submitted proof.\n  * info.json: the original verifier result and sample identifiers.\n  * final_messages.md: the submitting agent's closing notes.\n  * ANALYSIS.md: the earlier assessment; its literature claims are superseded by the dated literature review.\n\nThe run was `wikipedia-autoformalized-v-mn0wtzf7uda6y0dc`, sample `Conjecture.conjecture` (`CKVnjroniDKXpNNhBkDc9x`), completed on 5 September 2026. Its recorded score is C, claim proof, accepted by the comparator's default Lean kernel. The original theorem's generic name denotes the existence of a counterexample, not the universal conjecture. Its unused `.disproof := sorry` companion is excluded from the cleaned artifact.\n\n## About\n\nA Lean-verified candidate counterexample to the γ–θ conjecture in eternal domination.\n\n### Stars\n\n1 star\n\n### Watchers\n\n0 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n0 forks--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndependent sets in n-vertex k-chromatic ℓ-connected graphs - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X21000893)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; 10.1016/j.disc.2021.112376 ... We study the problem of maximizing the number of independent sets in n-vertex k-chromatic \\ell -connected graphs. ... Connectivity ... Independent set\n\n## Discrete Mathematics\n\nDate: July 2021\n\nArticle: 112376\n\nVolume: Volume 344, Issue 7\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Independent sets in n-vertex k-chromatic \\ell -connected graphs\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel John Engbers ^{a} ^{1}, Lauren Keough ^{b}, Taylor Short ^{b}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2021.112376\n\nMore actions\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study the problem of maximizing the number of independent sets in n-vertex k-chromatic \\ell -connected graphs. First we consider maximizing the total number of independent sets in such graphs with n sufficiently large, and for this problem we use a stability argument to find the unique extremal graph. We show that our result holds within the larger family of n-vertex k-chromatic graphs with minimum degree at least \\ell , again for n sufficiently large. We also maximize the number of independent sets of each fixed size in n-vertex 3-chromatic 2-connected graphs. We finally address maximizing the number of independent sets of size 2 (equivalently, minimizing the number of edges) over all n-vertex k-chromatic \\ell -connected graphs.\n\n## Keywords\n\nColoring\n\n;\n\nConnectivity\n\n;\n\nIndependent set\n\n;\n\nExtremal graph\n\n^{1}\n    \n\nResearch supported by the Simons Foundation, United States of America grant 524418.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExtremal colorings and independent sets (https://www.mssc.mu.edu/~john/Research/FixedChromNum.pdf)\nciteturn5search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; While Conjecture 1.1 is still open, it ... would also be interesting to consider the class of n-vertex k-chromatic claw-free connected graphs ... There are also plenty of questions related to independent sets. ... In our paper, we answer Question 4.1 in the aﬃrmative when ℓ= 0 (Theorem 1.6) and ℓ= 1 ... with n vertices and ﬁxed connectivity are studied in [18].\nwould also be interesting to consider the class of n-vertex k-chromatic claw-free connected graphs\nfor k > 4, and to show that the analogous statement to Theorem 1.2 holds for these k. It would also\nbe interesting to investigate the maximizing graphs for n-vertex k-chromatic ℓ-connected graphs for\nother values of ℓ.\nThere 